<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bibliotheca Palatina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bibliotheca Palatina was the library of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> palatine at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, housed in the Church of the Holy Spirit, and in the sixteenth century one of the finest collections of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> and books in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> troops took Heidelberg in 1622 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> presented it to Pope Gregory XV, and it was moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a> in 1623. Some 850 German manuscripts were returned to Heidelberg in 1816.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bibliotheca-palatina.jpg" alt="Bibliotheca Palatina" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Meister des Codex Manesse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altstetten.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the 1430s Elector Louis III founded the Stiftsbibliothek in the Heiliggeistkirche, chosen for its good reading light. This library formed the core of the Palatine collection that Elector Ottheinrich established in the 1550s together with the University Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>. At its height the Bibliotheca Palatina held about 5,000 printed books and 3,524 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and it was the most important library of the German Renaissance.</p><p>Among its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> were the Carolingian Lorsch Evangelary, the falconry book De arte venandi cum avibus commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-manesse">Codex Manesse</a>. Further manuscripts came from the collection of Ulrich Fugger, who died in 1584, among them the illustrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sachsenspiegel">Sachsenspiegel</a>. Joseph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scaliger">Scaliger</a> judged the Fugger library superior to the Pope's, and its manuscripts alone were valued at 80,000 crowns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1622 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> under Count von Tilly, who served Maximilian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. Book plundering was a source of both Catholic and Protestant cultural triumph in the war, and the occupiers competed for the library. Maximilian first wanted it for his own library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, while Ferdinand II sent Tilly orders to keep it for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. Many books were torn or dispersed among private hands during the sack, but Pope Gregory XV persuaded Maximilian to present the rest to the Vatican as a sign of loyalty and in support of his claim to the Palatinate's electoral title.</p><p>The Greek scholar Leo Allatius was sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to prepare the transport. The victors meant to strip the Calvinist party of one of its chief intellectual symbols and also to remove the records of the library's provenance, and the seizure set a pattern for later confiscations in the war. Each volume taken into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a> kept a leaf bearing the Wittelsbach arms.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tolentino">Treaty of Tolentino</a> of 1797 the Pope ceded 37 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> to the French Republic, which placed them in the Bibliothèque nationale in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> ordered the return of works of art taken by the French, these manuscripts went from Paris to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> rather than to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. In 1816 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-august-von-hardenberg">Prince Hardenberg</a> and Ignaz Heinrich von Wessenberg persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vii">Pope Pius VII</a> to give the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university">University of Heidelberg</a> 852 manuscripts, mostly in German. The German manuscripts carry shelf-marks beginning cpg and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> ones cpl. For the university's jubilee in 1986 some books were lent back from the Vatican and shown in the Heiliggeistkirche.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bibliotheca Palatina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Much of Heidelberg's great library, the <b>Bibliotheca Palatina</b>, was sent to Rome in 1623 as a gift to the pope after the city fell, and it remains in the Vatican Library.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Electoral Palatinate · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
